#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/id #uuid "671da5f4-e539-588e-9bec-81c71e016d2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5104" :question/problem "In a class, each student selected one course among the two possible options, math or physics. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Female | $12$ | $12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $20$ | $6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male (or both)." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male, we\n\n- add up the numbers that are in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Female | $12$ | $\\color{blue}12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50)$, we get\n\n$$\np =\\dfrac{12+20+6}{50} = \\dfrac{38}{50} =0.76\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054662 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events"}] :question/id #uuid "ec0a90ec-7cd7-5ab5-abb2-9e33cbd4b711" :question/math-academy-id "e-5105" :question/problem "Anna surveys some students in a school, asking them what pets out of cats or dogs they have at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Has a cat | $30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nLet $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a cat\"**, and $D$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a dog\"**. Estimate $P(C \\cap D)$." :question/worked-solution "The event $C \\cap D$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the school has both a cat and a dog.\n\nTo estimate the probability of $C \\cap D$, we need to\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Has a cat | $\\color{blue}30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(200)$, we get\n\n$$\nP(C \\cap D) = \\dfrac{30}{200} = 0.15\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/id #uuid "f09f68d2-f44f-5328-963b-41a9884a944a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5106" :question/problem "There are $250$ boys and $250$ girls in a school. Of them, $40$ boys and $40$ girls were randomly selected and asked which sport they prefer between volleyball, basketball, football, or soccer. The results of the survey are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $11$ | $9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected student in the survey is a boy who prefers football or soccer, and then use this to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer, we\n\n- add up the numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $\\color{blue}11$ | $\\color{blue}9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80)$, we get\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{11+9}{80} = \\dfrac{20}{80} = 0.25\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer.\n\nFinally, to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 500 \\cdot p \\\\\n&= 500 \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cca7bbd7-fb2c-50b2-babf-c466777230b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-46814" :question/problem "Martin surveyed some students in a school, asking them if they have cats or dogs at home. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\" Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(32),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9+10}{32}=\\frac{19}{32}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054663 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating Using Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af2f72a0-8f30-5a0e-a022-a97a7f52f774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053212} {:answer/id #uuid "d1817f58-64f5-546d-a957-b253a7f8da60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053213} {:answer/id #uuid "fe64e4fa-548b-5090-aefd-9533e1e17236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053214} {:answer/id #uuid "1af1780d-1f06-5e56-81bc-bfccd4223b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053215} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd3bbf4-e126-5de2-9d39-f96423983718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053216}] :answer-field/id #uuid "16caa5bd-0c6c-52c9-be37-956a1d8a8e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053211}] :question/id #uuid "aa1f968b-ce45-555d-acfc-8866cb45d38d" :question/math-academy-id "q-47138" :question/problem "A group of randomly selected students from School A was asked about whether or not they use TV or the internet to access the local news. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nThere are $100$ students in School B. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both schools,\nestimate the number of School B students that access the local news either from TV or the internet, but not both.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054661 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f5c3b02-3757-5ac4-8689-9a0642cbbf9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053224} {:answer/id #uuid "91c02db5-84de-5acc-abef-97f62ab3cd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053225} {:answer/id #uuid "92959582-544d-51a2-9941-734879b6e119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053226} {:answer/id #uuid "27190c4d-66f9-539b-b083-e122239eba9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053227} {:answer/id #uuid "5434afcf-de9a-5ac6-a3f1-0a118a94fca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053228}] :answer-field/id #uuid "baeac06b-4ef9-51f2-94b1-0894733ab9a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053223}] :question/id #uuid "66372d89-3abf-597d-98d0-70064323b74c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47141" :question/problem "In a high school, a group of students was asked about whether they prefer apples, oranges, or bananas. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Apple | Orange | Banana | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girls | $11$ | $8$ | $7$ | $26$ |\n| Boys | $12$ | $6$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $14$ | $13$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the group preferred apples or bananas or is a girl.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
